{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23074","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23074","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23074","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiromu","familyName":"Takahashi","name":"Takahashi, Hiromu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shotaro","familyName":"Ishihara","name":"Ishihara, Shotaro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fast-MIA: Efficient and Scalable Membership Inference for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T07:18:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:26:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-10T11:35:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-12T01:14:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Fast-MIA (https://github.com/Nikkei/fast-mia), a Python library for efficiently evaluating membership inference attacks (MIA) against large language models (LLMs). MIA has emerged as a crucial technique for auditing privacy risks and copyright infringement in LLMs. However, computational demands have grown substantially: recent methods rely on repeated inference, while practical auditing requires large-scale evaluation. Progress is further hindered by existing implementations that execute methods independently, redundantly computing shared intermediate results such as log-probabilities. To address these challenges, Fast-MIA combines two strategies: (1) high-throughput batch inference via vLLM, achieving approximately 5$\\times$ speedup, and (2) a cross-method caching architecture that computes intermediate results once and shares them across methods. The library includes representative MIA methods under a unified framework, integrates with established benchmarks, and supports flexible YAML configuration. We release Fast-MIA under the Apache License 2.0 to support scalable and reproducible MIA research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026 System Demonstrations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23074","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:05:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faria","familyName":"Huq","name":"Huq, Faria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elijah L.","familyName":"Claggett","name":"Claggett, Elijah L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hirokazu","familyName":"Shirado","name":"Shirado, Hirokazu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI-Mediated Communication Reshapes Social Structure in Opinion-Diverse Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T19:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:10:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-25T05:16:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:29:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Group segregation or cohesion can emerge from micro-level communication, and AI-assisted messaging may shape this process. Here, we report a preregistered online experiment (N = 557 across 60 sessions) in which participants discussed controversial political topics over multiple rounds and could freely change groups. Some participants received real-time message suggestions from a large language model (LLM), either personalized to their stance (individual assistance) or incorporating their group members' perspectives (relational assistance). We find that small variations in AI-mediated communication cascade into macro-level differences in group composition. Participants with individual assistance send more messages and show greater stance-based clustering, whereas those with relational assistance use more receptive language and form more heterogeneous ties. Hybrid expressive processes-jointly produced by humans and AI-can reshape collective organization. The patterns of structural division and cohesion depend on how AI incorporates users' interaction context."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:04:33Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:04:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-26T03:25:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binxiao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Binxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaolin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shilin","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Jarvis: Towards Personalized AI Assistant via Personal KV-Cache Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:28:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T07:01:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:23:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid development of Vision-language models (VLMs) enables open-ended perception and reasoning. Recent works have started to investigate how to adapt general-purpose VLMs into personalized assistants. Even commercial models such as ChatGPT now support model personalization by incorporating user-specific information. However, existing methods either learn a set of concept tokens or train a VLM to utilize user-specific information. However, both pipelines struggle to generate accurate answers as personalized assistants. We introduce Jarvis, an innovative framework for a personalized AI assistant through personal KV-Cache retrieval, which stores user-specific information in the KV-Caches of both textual and visual tokens. The textual tokens are created by summarizing user information into metadata, while the visual tokens are produced by extracting distinct image patches from the user's images. When answering a question, Jarvis first retrieves related KV-Caches from personal storage and uses them to ensure accuracy in responses. We also introduce a fine-grained benchmark built with the same distinct image patch mining pipeline, emphasizing accurate question answering based on fine-grained user-specific information. Jarvis is capable of providing more accurate responses, particularly when they depend on specific local details. Jarvis achieves state-of-the-art results in both visual question answering and text-only tasks across multiple datasets, indicating a practical path toward personalized AI assistants. The code and dataset will be released."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T05:00:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23264","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23264","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23264","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huanyi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Huanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Lijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PAHQ: Accelerating Automated Circuit Discovery through Mixed-Precision Inference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:24:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:40:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Circuit discovery, which involves identifying sparse and task-relevant subnetworks in pre-trained language models, is a cornerstone of mechanistic interpretability. Automated Circuit Discovery (ACDC) has emerged as a pivotal methodology in circuit discovery, but its application to large language models is severely limited by computational inefficiency and prohibitively high memory requirements. Although several accelerated approaches have been proposed, they primarily rely on linear approximations to ACDC, which significantly compromises analytical faithfulness. Our proposed method for accelerating automated circuit discovery, Per Attention Head Quantization (PAHQ), takes a fundamentally different approach by optimizing the efficiency of each individual patching operation. PAHQ leverages a fundamental alignment between activation patching and mixed-precision quantization (MPQ): interpretability analysis through patching essentially performs targeted ablation studies. Therefore, we can maintain high precision exclusively for investigated components while safely reducing precision elsewhere in the network. PAHQ-accelerated ACDC reduces runtime by up to 80\\% and memory consumption by up to 30\\% compared to unaccelerated ACDC while maintaining faithfulness. Importantly, our method readily integrates with existing edge-based circuit discovery techniques by modifying the attention computation mechanism. This training-free approach provides a practical and novel pathway for accelerating mechanistic interpretability methods. Our code is available at https://github.com/626619403/PAHQ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23264","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:57Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:34:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Henneke","name":"Henneke, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Kurth","name":"Kurth, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Approaching Domain Generalization with Embeddings for Robust Discrimination and Recognition of RF Communication Signals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T10:24:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:34:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Radio frequency (RF) signal recognition plays a critical role in modern wireless communication and security applications. Deep learning-based approaches have achieved strong performance but typically rely heavily on extensive training data and often fail to generalize to unseen signals. In this paper, we propose a method to learn discriminative embeddings without relying on real-world RF signal recordings by training on signals of synthetic wireless protocols. We validate the approach on a dataset of real RF signals and show that the learned embeddings capture features enabling accurate discrimination of previously unseen real-world signals, highlighting its potential for robust RF signal classification and anomaly detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:33:06Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:33:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:33:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongshun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zongshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Matta","name":"Matta, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Inference Placement for Deep Learning across Edge and Cloud Platforms: A Multi-Objective Optimization Perspective and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T01:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:15:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Edge intelligent applications like VR/AR and language model based chatbots have become widespread with the rapid expansion of IoT and mobile devices. However, constrained edge devices often cannot serve the increasingly large and complex deep learning (DL) models. To mitigate these challenges, researchers have proposed optimizing and offloading partitions of DL models among user devices, edge servers, and the cloud. In this setting, users can take advantage of different services to support their intelligent applications. For example, edge resources offer low response latency. In contrast, cloud platforms provide low monetary cost computation resources for computation-intensive workloads. However, communication between DL model partitions can introduce transmission bottlenecks and pose risks of data leakage. Recent research aims to balance accuracy, computation delay, transmission delay, and privacy concerns. They address these issues with model compression, model distillation, transmission compression, and model architecture adaptations, including internal classifiers. This survey contextualizes the state-of-the-art model offloading methods and model adaptation techniques by studying their implication to a multi-objective optimization comprising inference latency, data privacy, and resource monetary cost."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:27Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:26:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne","familyName":"Arriola","name":"Arriola, Marianne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yair","familyName":"Schiff","name":"Schiff, Yair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Phung","name":"Phung, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Gokaslan","name":"Gokaslan, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volodymyr","familyName":"Kuleshov","name":"Kuleshov, Volodymyr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T22:05:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete diffusion models enable parallel token sampling for faster inference than autoregressive approaches. However, prior diffusion models use a decoder-only architecture, which requires sampling algorithms that invoke the full network at every denoising step and incur high computational cost. Our key insight is that discrete diffusion models perform two types of computation: 1) representing clean tokens and 2) denoising corrupted tokens, which enables us to use separate modules for each task. We propose an encoder-decoder architecture to accelerate discrete diffusion inference, which relies on an encoder to represent clean tokens and a lightweight decoder to iteratively refine a noised sequence. We also show that this architecture enables faster training of block diffusion models, which partition sequences into blocks for better quality and are commonly used in diffusion language model inference. We introduce a framework for Efficient Encoder-Decoder Diffusion (E2D2), consisting of an architecture with specialized training and sampling algorithms, and we show that E2D2 achieves superior trade-offs between generation quality and inference throughput on summarization, translation, and mathematical reasoning tasks. We provide the code, model weights, and blog post on the project page: https://m-arriola.com/e2d2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. We provide the code at https://github.com/kuleshov-group/e2d2"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22763","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22763","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22763","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasmin","familyName":"Moslem","name":"Moslem, Yasmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Hazim Al","familyName":"Farouq","name":"Farouq, Muhammad Hazim Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John D.","familyName":"Kelleher","name":"Kelleher, John D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Iterative Layer Pruning for Efficient Translation Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:26:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2025.wmt-1.78","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have transformed many areas of natural language processing, including machine translation. However, efficient deployment of LLMs remains challenging due to their intensive computational requirements. In this paper, we address this challenge and present our submissions to the Model Compression track at the Conference on Machine Translation (WMT 2025). In our experiments, we investigate iterative layer pruning guided by layer importance analysis. We evaluate this method using the Aya-Expanse-8B model for translation from Czech to German, and from English to Egyptian Arabic. Our approach achieves substantial reductions in model size and inference time, while maintaining the translation quality of the baseline models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"WMT 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22763","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:01Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:23:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divya Jyoti","familyName":"Bajpai","name":"Bajpai, Divya Jyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manjesh Kumar","familyName":"Hanawal","name":"Hanawal, Manjesh Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FastVLM: Self-Speculative Decoding for Fast Vision-Language Model Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T11:49:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:59:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language Models (VLMs) have made significant strides in visual understanding and query response generation, but often face challenges of high computational cost and inference latency due to autoregressive decoding. In this work, we introduce an imitation-learning-based Self-Speculative Decoding (SSD) framework, named FastVLM, to address these limitations. Our approach employs a lightweight draft model for token generation in an autoregressive manner, while a full model verifies these tokens non-autoregressively. Accepted tokens proceed seamlessly, while rejected tokens are corrected by the full model and used to guide the draft model's refinement. Through an imitation network, FastVLM enhances the draft model by integrating deeper level insights from the full model's architecture. Also, it maintains the performance integrity of the full model while training the draft model, achieving a balance between efficiency and accuracy. Our method speeds up the inference process by 1.55-1.85x as compared to the final layer with minimal loss in performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for presentation at the main Conference IJCNLP-AACL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22556","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22556","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22556","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jinhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianchun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jianchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongli","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hongli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianjun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xianjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shilong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SABlock: Semantic-Aware KV Cache Eviction with Adaptive Compression Block Size"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T07:17:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:53:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The growing memory footprint of the Key-Value (KV) cache poses a severe scalability bottleneck for long-context Large Language Model (LLM) inference. While KV cache eviction has emerged as an effective solution by discarding less critical tokens, existing token-, block-, and sentence-level compression methods struggle to balance semantic coherence and memory efficiency. To this end, we introduce SABlock, a \\underline{s}emantic-aware KV cache eviction framework with \\underline{a}daptive \\underline{block} sizes. Specifically, SABlock first performs semantic segmentation to align compression boundaries with linguistic structures, then applies segment-guided token scoring to refine token importance estimation. Finally, for each segment, a budget-driven search strategy adaptively determines the optimal block size that preserves semantic integrity while improving compression efficiency under a given cache budget. Extensive experiments on long-context benchmarks demonstrate that SABlock consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines under the same memory budgets. For instance, on Needle-in-a-Haystack (NIAH), SABlock achieves 99.9% retrieval accuracy with only 96 KV entries, nearly matching the performance of the full-cache baseline that retains up to 8K entries. Under a fixed cache budget of 1,024, SABlock further reduces peak memory usage by 46.28% and achieves up to 9.5x faster decoding on a 128K context length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22556","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:18:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:18:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:18:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjue","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiangjue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Teleki","name":"Teleki, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Millennium","familyName":"Bismay","name":"Bismay, Millennium","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Caverlee","name":"Caverlee, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CHOIR: Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T01:20:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:47:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Persona-assigned Large Language Models (LLMs) can adopt diverse roles, enabling personalized and context-aware reasoning. However, even minor demographic perturbations in personas, such as simple pronoun changes, can alter reasoning trajectories, leading to divergent sets of correct answers. Instead of treating these variations as biases to be mitigated, we explore their potential as a constructive resource to improve reasoning robustness. We propose CHOIR (Collaborative Harmonization fOr Inference Robustness), a test-time framework that harmonizes multiple persona-conditioned reasoning signals into a unified prediction. CHOIR orchestrates a collaborative decoding process among counterfactual personas, dynamically balancing agreement and divergence in their reasoning paths. Experiments on various reasoning benchmarks demonstrate that CHOIR consistently enhances performance across demographics, model architectures, scales, and tasks - without additional training. Improvements reach up to 26.4% for individual demographic groups and 19.2% on average across five demographics. It remains effective even when base personas are suboptimal. By reframing persona variation as a constructive signal, CHOIR provides a scalable and generalizable approach to more reliable LLM reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"updated version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:16:15Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:16:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:16:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Veerabadran","name":"Veerabadran, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanyi","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitin","familyName":"Kamra","name":"Kamra, Nitin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Matias","name":"Matias, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caley","familyName":"Drooff","name":"Drooff, Caley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brett D","familyName":"Roads","name":"Roads, Brett D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riley","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Riley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Carlberg","name":"Carlberg, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Tighe","name":"Tighe, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl","familyName":"Ridgeway","name":"Ridgeway, Karl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Egocentric Multimodal Goal Inference for Assistive Wearable Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T21:54:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:44:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There has been a surge of interest in assistive wearable agents: agents embodied in wearable form factors (e.g., smart glasses) who take assistive actions toward a user's goal/query (e.g. \"Where did I leave my keys?\"). In this work, we consider the important complementary problem of inferring that goal from multi-modal contextual observations. Solving this \"goal inference\" problem holds the promise of eliminating the effort needed to interact with such an agent. This work focuses on creating WAGIBench, a strong benchmark to measure progress in solving this problem using vision-language models (VLMs). Given the limited prior work in this area, we collected a novel dataset comprising 29 hours of multimodal data from 348 participants across 3,477 recordings, featuring ground-truth goals alongside accompanying visual, audio, digital, and longitudinal contextual observations. We validate that human performance exceeds model performance, achieving 93% multiple-choice accuracy compared with 84% for the best-performing VLM. Generative benchmark results that evaluate several families of modern vision-language models show that larger models perform significantly better on the task, yet remain far from practical usefulness, as they produce relevant goals only 55% of the time. Through a modality ablation, we show that models benefit from extra information in relevant modalities with minimal performance degradation from irrelevant modalities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a spotlight paper at the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhepeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhepeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CGoT: A Novel Inference Mechanism for Embodied Multi-Agent Systems Using Composable Graphs of Thoughts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T09:39:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:32:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The integration of self-driving cars and service robots is becoming increasingly prevalent across a wide array of fields, playing a crucial and expanding role in both industrial applications and everyday life. In parallel, the rapid advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have garnered substantial attention and interest within the research community. This paper introduces a novel vehicle-robot system that leverages the strengths of both autonomous vehicles and service robots. In our proposed system, two autonomous ego-vehicles transports service robots to locations within an office park, where they perform a series of tasks. The study explores the feasibility and potential benefits of incorporating LLMs into this system, with the aim of enhancing operational efficiency and maximizing the potential of the cooperative mechanisms between the vehicles and the robots. This paper proposes a novel inference mechanism which is called CGOT toward this type of system where an agent can carry another agent. Experimental results are presented to validate the performance of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:10:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:10:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:10:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kayhan","familyName":"Behdin","name":"Behdin, Kayhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingquan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Qingquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriram","familyName":"Vasudevan","name":"Vasudevan, Sriram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojing","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xiaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Z","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanrui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chuanrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoyao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guoyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanh","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Chanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayan","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Sayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hejian","familyName":"Sang","name":"Sang, Hejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ata Fatahi","familyName":"Baarzi","name":"Baarzi, Ata Fatahi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sundara Raman","familyName":"Ramachandran","name":"Ramachandran, Sundara Raman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinay Y","familyName":"S","name":"S, Vinay Y","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caleb","familyName":"Johnson","name":"Johnson, Caleb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fedor","familyName":"Borisyuk","name":"Borisyuk, Fedor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Up Efficient Small Language Models Serving and Deployment for Semantic Job Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T00:56:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:20:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated impressive quality when applied to predictive tasks such as relevance ranking and semantic search. However, deployment of such LLMs remains prohibitively expensive for industry applications with strict latency and throughput requirements. In this work, we present lessons and efficiency insights from developing a purely text-based decoder-only Small Language Model (SLM) for a semantic search application at LinkedIn. Particularly, we discuss model compression techniques such as pruning that allow us to reduce the model size by up to $40\\%$ while maintaining the accuracy. Additionally, we present context compression techniques that allow us to reduce the input context length by up to $10$x with minimal loss of accuracy. Finally, we present practical lessons from optimizing the serving infrastructure for deploying such a system on GPUs at scale, serving millions of requests per second. Taken together, this allows us to increase our system's throughput by $10$x in a real-world deployment, while meeting our quality bar."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:07:18Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:07:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:07:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21956","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21956","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21956","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armin","familyName":"Gerami","name":"Gerami, Armin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramani","familyName":"Duraiswami","name":"Duraiswami, Ramani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Transformer Based Linear Attention with Optimized GPU Kernel Implementation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T18:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:07:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The original softmax-based attention mechanism (regular attention) in the extremely successful Transformer architecture computes attention between $N$ tokens, each embedded in a $D$-dimensional head, with a time complexity of $O(N^2D)$. Given the success of Transformers, improving their runtime during both training and inference is a popular research area. One such approach is the introduction of the linear attention (LA) mechanisms, which offers a linear time complexity of $O(ND^2)$ and have demonstrated comparable accuracy to regular attention. However, LA in practice lags behind its theoretical efficiency. We propose a novel method for LA's forward and backward passes, along with a highly-optimized CUDA implementation. Our approach outperforms the state-of-the-art by 3.3 times in speed and reduces memory consumption by 3.6 times. We validate these improvements in both single-layer and end-to-end settings by training a 1.4 billion parameter language model, which demonstrates similar expressivity to regular attention on major reasoning benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21956","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:03:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:03:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:03:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kejia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiacong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunlei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingli","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Mingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token-Level Inference-Time Alignment for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-20T09:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have become essential backbones of modern multimodal intelligence, yet their outputs remain prone to hallucination-plausible text misaligned with visual inputs. Existing alignment approaches often rely on expensive fine-tuning with annotated preference data or sequence-level inference strategies that provide only coarse, delayed feedback. To overcome these limitations, we present TITA (Token-level Inference-Time Alignment), a lightweight framework that freezes the base VLM and instead trains a reward model to approximate its distribution. During inference, implicit preference signals are extracted as log-probability ratios between the reward model and the target VLM, yielding dense autoregressive feedback. This formulation can be viewed as an inference-time variant of Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), providing token-level corrective signals without retraining the backbone. Extensive evaluations on LLaVA-1.5-7B and 13B show consistent gains across 12 benchmarks, with improvements of 8.6% on MMVet and 6.7% on POPE, indicating stronger general understanding and reduced hallucinations. Additional experiments on Qwen2.5-VL-7B and DeepSeek-VL2-27.5B show comparable gains, especially in hallucination reduction and VQA accuracy, while incurring negligible inference overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:05Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harris","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Harris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Avi: Action from Volumetric Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T23:10:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:01:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose Avi, a novel 3D Vision-Language-Action (VLA) architecture that reframes robotic action generation as a problem of 3D perception and spatial reasoning, rather than low-level policy learning. While existing VLA models primarily operate on 2D visual inputs and are trained end-to-end on task-specific action policies, Avi leverages 3D point clouds and language-grounded scene understanding to compute actions through classical geometric transformations. Most notably, Avi does not train on previous action tokens, rather, we build upon a 3D Multi-modal Large Language Model (MLLM) to generate the next point cloud and explicitly calculate the actions through classical transformations. This approach enables generalizable behaviors that are robust to occlusions, camera pose variations, and changes in viewpoint. By treating the robotic decision-making process as a structured reasoning task over 3D representations, Avi bridges the gap between high-level language instructions and low-level actuation without requiring opaque policy learning. Our preliminary results highlight the potential of 3D vision-language reasoning as a foundation for scalable, robust robotic systems. Check it out at https://avi-3drobot.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Embodied World Models for Decision Making. URL: https://avi-3drobot.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T02:58:57Z","registered":"2025-10-28T02:58:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T02:58:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":17,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-27T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-28T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+transformer+OR+%22deep+learning%22%29+AND+titles.title%3A%28inference+OR+serving+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+cache+OR+parallel+OR+quantization+OR+compiler+OR+accelerator+OR+communication+OR+scheduling%29%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}